+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "b59f9b17",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Wrapper names\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The C++ factor is templated on the Euclidean point dimension `N`. Python exposes the 2D and 3D instantiations `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor2` and `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor3`, respectively."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "f5f6e07c",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Prepare one known-landmark range measurement\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Each factor needs four inputs:\n",
+    "\n",
+    "1. the point key `k`;\n",
+    "2. a fixed world-frame landmark `wL`;\n",
+    "3. the measured range `R`; and\n",
+    "4. a noise model describing uncertainty in the squared-range residual.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "The residual $e$ is a scalar, so the corresponding noise model is one-dimensional, with scalar standard deviation $\\sigma$ and covariance $\\sigma^2$.\n"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 7,
+   "id": "6e719445",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "k = 0\n",
+    "wL = np.array([2.0, 3.0, 6.0])\n",
+    "R = 7.1\n",
+    "\n",
+    "sigma = 0.1  # squared-range residual standard deviation [m^2]\n",
+    "model = gtsam.noiseModel.Isotropic.Sigma(1, sigma)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "ef50107e",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Localization with `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor`\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Add the factor to an ordinary nonlinear graph and insert an initial point estimate under the same key."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 8,
+   "id": "321197fb",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "graph = gtsam.NonlinearFactorGraph()\n",
+    "graph.add(\n",
+    "    gtsam.KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor3(k, wL, R, model)\n",
+    ")\n",
+    "\n",
+    "wX_initial = np.array([-0.1, 0.2, -0.05])\n",
+    "initial_wXs = gtsam.Values()\n",
+    "initial_wXs.insert(k, wX_initial)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "7b454e9f",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Build a well-constrained graph\n",
+    "\n",
+    "A single range measurement does not uniquely determine the point state. In 2D, it constrains the point to a circle centered at the known landmark; in 3D, it constrains the point to a sphere. In a real localization graph, add factors for several distinct known landmarks and, where appropriate, point priors or other factors. Each observation should carry its own `R` and noise model. Reusing the same point key connects all observations made from that point.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "A known landmark is fixed factor data, not a variable in `Values`."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "32acc07c",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Practical checklist\n",
+    "\n",
+    "- Use `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor2` or `KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeFactor3` for point localization from ranges to fixed, known landmarks.\n",
+    "- Express the state point and landmark in the same coordinate frame.\n",
+    "- Pass the ordinary non-negative measured range `R`; the factor squares it internally.\n",
+    "- Supply a one-dimensional noise model, since the squared-range residual is scalar.\n",
+    "- Interpret the noise model as uncertainty on the squared-range residual, not directly on the range measurement.\n",
+    "- Add measurements from enough geometrically distinct landmarks, or other factors, to fully constrain the point state.\n",
+    "- Keep known landmarks as fixed factor data; they are not variables in `Values`.\n",
+    "\n",
+    "## Related documentation\n",
+    "\n",
+    "- [KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeLocalizationExample](../../../python/gtsam/examples/slam/KnownLandmarkSquaredRangeLocalizationExample.ipynb) demonstrates 3D point localization from noisy range measurements to fixed known landmarks.\n",
+    "- [KnownLandmarkFactor](KnownLandmarkFactor.ipynb) documents Cartesian observations of fixed known landmarks using pose states."
+   ]
+  }
